1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
What sets apart China's vision for the future of the world?

2
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,439
China has just offered more answers to that question.

3
00:00:42,439 --> 00:00:48,439
A white paper has been released on Wednesday in Beijing titled More Just and Equitable

4
00:00:48,439 --> 00:00:53,840
Global Governance – China's Principles, Proposals and Actions.

5
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:59,120
This document fleshes out a proposal Chinese President Xi Jinping put forward last September

6
00:00:59,239 --> 00:01:03,599
called the Global Governance Initiative or GGI.

7
00:01:03,599 --> 00:01:09,120
With that, China has already proposed a complete action framework made up of four different

8
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:13,479
initiatives laying out the country's vision for the future of the world.

9
00:01:13,479 --> 00:01:17,560
So why is China releasing this white paper now?

10
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:22,640
What does it actually say and how might it affect you who are watching us now?

11
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:28,640
Welcome to this special edition of The Point with me, Liu Xin, coming to you live from the Chinese capital.

12
00:01:29,159 --> 00:01:34,719
I'm pleased to be joined from Shanghai by Claire Pearson, International Development

13
00:01:34,719 --> 00:01:42,719
Director of the global law firm DLA Piper and former chair of the British Chamber of Commons in China.

14
00:01:42,719 --> 00:01:49,799
From Beijing by Yasiru Rana Raja, founding director at the Belt and Road Initiative for

15
00:01:49,799 --> 00:01:51,799
Sri Lanka.

16
00:01:51,799 --> 00:01:57,359
And here in the studio by Lu Xiang, research fellow at the Institute of American Studies

17
00:01:57,359 --> 00:02:00,120
of the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences.

18
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,760
The warmest welcome to all of you.

19
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:06,719
So first of all, Claire, it's good to have you on the show.

20
00:02:06,719 --> 00:02:10,759
You always make things so easy and interesting to listen to.

21
00:02:10,759 --> 00:02:16,199
So why does it matter for the people who are watching us now that we talk about GGI and

22
00:02:16,199 --> 00:02:22,759
the other series of global initiatives that China has proposed?

23
00:02:22,759 --> 00:02:24,259
Nice to see you, Liu Xin.

24
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,439
Thank you very much for having me on the show.

25
00:02:26,439 --> 00:02:32,840
The reason I think that GGI really matters at the moment is because the world is in a state of flux.

26
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:39,039
So if you think about the international institutions that were developed under Bretton Woods, IMF,

27
00:02:39,039 --> 00:02:47,159
or subsequently United Nations, that was 1944, 1945, a very idealistic period of rebuilding

28
00:02:47,159 --> 00:02:49,039
after the war.

29
00:02:49,039 --> 00:02:53,479
And without a doubt, the countries that dominated those discussions were the ones that were

30
00:02:53,599 --> 00:02:57,879
the economic and geopolitical hegemons at the time.

31
00:02:57,879 --> 00:02:59,879
The US was responsible for 35 percent of the world's economic and geopolitical hegemons.
